# Git workflow

Never commit feature work straight to `main`.

For every new feature or fix:

1. Create a fresh branch from latest `main`.
   - Features: `feat/short-name`
   - Fixes: `fix/short-name`
2. Make commits only on that branch.
3. Push the branch, open a PR into `main`.
4. Merge the PR after it is opened (squash merge unless asked otherwise).
5. Delete the branch after merge.

PR title: short, like a student writing for a teacher. One line.

PR body: 2–4 short lines. What changed and why. No buzzwords, no emoji, no “summary / test plan” fluff unless the user asked for a GitHub template fill.

# Commit messages

Write like a final year college student: clear, a bit casual, still professional.

Do:
- One short sentence (or two max)
- Say what you actually did
- Lowercase is fine

Don't:
- Sound like ChatGPT (“implemented a robust solution”, “enhances maintainability”)
- Use conventional-commit prefixes (`feat:`, `chore:`) unless the user asks
- Write an essay or bullet novel

Examples:
- `added signup and login apis with jwt`
- `fixed pan regex failing on lowercase input`
- `wired disbursement page to mark loan as disbursed`
